068 #edb/ref 17592186410707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410714 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410714 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410714 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410714 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f92504f-2a88-577a-b7ee-007131f73594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410714 #edb/ref 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nThis gives rise to the following facts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,⟺\\,a≡b+kn\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ a≡b\\,⟺\\,a+kn≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can add a multiple of the modulus $15$ to one side of the congruence. Notice that \n\n$$\n-45=(-3)\\cdot 15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-45≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
